@implements IAsyncDisposable
@using Microsoft.JSInterop
@inject InboxDesktopState State
@inject IJSRuntime Js

@*
    The Inbox pane: what has been captured but not yet decided on, read the way
    a To Do app reads — lists down the side, the rows of the open list in the
    middle, the chosen row's detail on the right. The pane has no idea a backlog
    row exists: "Move to backlog" is an act the Inbox's own module performs
    through a port, and the shell hears about it afterwards through the state's
    Routed event. That is what keeps triage a decision the Inbox owns rather
    than a view of someone else's list.

    Lists and groups are the structure, and they are the reader's: a starter set
    is seeded on first start and everything after that is made, renamed, filed
    and deleted from the side menu's own context menu. The fixed "Inbox" row is
    the one list nobody made — what has been captured and not yet filed.

    Kinds are a filter, never a section. The rows are grouped by how long ago
    they arrived — Today, This week, Older than a week — and the pills above
    them narrow the groups to the videos, or the articles, or both. A kind is a
    fact about a capture; a list is where it goes.

    Three child components rather than one file, split where the state splits:
    the side menu draws lists and groups, the queue's health at its foot, and
    owns the menus that change them; the item list draws the slice and the
    pills; the detail draws one item and the acts on it. All three read the one
    state object the shell registers, which is what lets the pane be re-mounted
    in another slot without forgetting what was open — the same bargain
    TasksDesktopState makes.

    The header is one line: the title with the queue's short health beside it,
    the capture field, and the pane's four acts. The capture field is how a
    thing the reader has in their head right now gets in — always open, Enter
    files the title, Shift+Enter opens notes under it, Ctrl+Enter files both,
    Escape closes the notes — and it goes through the state into the Inbox's
    module as an item, body and all, with nothing outside the Inbox learning of
    it. Capture now runs the sources the reader has asked to be watched; it is
    raised to the host and nothing more — which sources, and what a run is,
    belongs to the Capture context, and the run's outcome comes back as a
    sentence so the pane never learns that context's types either.

    The sources Capture now looks at are configured on this pane too, because a
    switch three screens from its button is a switch nobody finds — but not by
    this pane. It offers a slot and the shell fills it with the Capture
    context's own panel, so the pane draws a region it cannot name, the same
    way it raises a run it cannot describe. The header's Sources button swaps
    the pane's body for that region, with "Back to Inbox" over it: the sources
    are set once and read in full, and a strip over the rows was too short for
    either.

    The old pane took its items from the shell, which is why the shell knew what
    an inbox item looked like. This one asks its own module; the four
    parameters it does take are the Capture button's and its sources slot, and
    the shell renders it with those alone.

    Triage is meant to be short, so it runs from the keyboard: j and k move
    through the rows, a archives, d defers, l moves to a list, r routes, g goes
    to the tags, t starts triage, x picks, u undoes the last decision, ? lists
    them all. Whether a key is a shortcut or a letter being typed is decided in
    components.js at the keydown — a key in a field, a dialog, or with a modifier held types — because a
    preventDefault rendered from here arrives one key late. Triage mode swaps
    the columns for one item at full width with "12 of 40" over it; each
    decision moves on, and Escape or "Back to the list" returns to the rows on
    the item it stopped at. The Tasks pane binds no letters, so the one key the
    two share is Escape, which in both leaves the mode the reader is in.
*@

<section @key=@("inbox-pane") class="inbox-pane" id="inbox-pane" aria-labelledby="inbox-pane-title" data-testid="inbox-pane">
    @* The library's section header wearing this pane's own class names: the
       title with the queue's short health as its description, and the capture
       field and the acts in its slot. All of it is drawn whether or not the
       queue has anything in it: an empty Inbox is exactly where somebody
       reaches for the field. *@
    <SectionHeader BaseClass="inbox-pane__header"
                   TextCssClass="inbox-pane__heading"
                   EyebrowCssClass="inbox-pane__eyebrow"
                   TitleCssClass="inbox-pane__title"
                   DescriptionCssClass="inbox-pane__subtitle"
                   DescriptionTestId="inbox-pane-waiting"
                   ActionsCssClass="inbox-pane__header-actions"
                   TitleId="inbox-pane-title"
                   Title="Inbox"
                   Description="@(State.Loaded ? State.WaitingSummary : null)">
        <Actions>
            <div class="inbox-pane__capture" data-testid="inbox-pane-capture-bar">
                <svg class="inbox-pane__capture-mark" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"></path></svg>
                <TextField AriaLabel="Capture a thought"
                           Placeholder="Write something down — Enter files it in Inbox"
                           Value="@_title"
                           ValueChanged="v => _title = v"
                           Id="@CaptureInputId"
                           BaseClass="inbox-pane__capture-field"
                           InputCssClass="inbox-pane__capture-input"
                           TestId="inbox-pane-capture-field"
                           autocomplete="off"
                           aria-describedby="inbox-pane-capture-hint"
                           @onkeydown="OnCaptureKeyDownAsync" />
                <span class="inbox-pane__capture-keys" id="inbox-pane-capture-hint">
                    <kbd class="inbox-pane__key">Enter</kbd>
                    <kbd class="inbox-pane__key">Shift+Enter adds notes</kbd>
                </span>
            </div>
            @* Busy rather than hidden while a run is in flight, so focus survives
               the round trip and a second press cannot start a second run. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       TestId="inbox-pane-capture"
                       CssClass="inbox-pane__header-act"
                       Busy="@CaptureRunning"
                       BusyLabel="Capturing"
                       Title="Run every watched source now"
                       OnClick="() => OnCapture.InvokeAsync()">
                <svg class="inbox-pane__icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M21 12a9 9 0 1 1-3-6.7L21 8"></path><path d="M21 3v5h-5"></path></svg>
                Capture now
            </AppButton>
            @if (Sources is not null)
            {
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                           Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                           TestId="inbox-pane-sources-open"
                           CssClass="inbox-pane__header-act"
                           Title="The sources Capture now watches"
                           aria-pressed="@(SourcesShown ? "true" : "false")"
                           OnClick="() => State.ShowSources(!State.SourcesShown)">
                    <svg class="inbox-pane__icon" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 11a9 9 0 0 1 9 9"></path><path d="M4 4a16 16 0 0 1 16 16"></path><circle cx="5" cy="19" r="1"></circle></svg>
                    Sources
                </AppButton>
            }
            @* The keys, where a first-time reader looks for what a pane can do:
               beside its other actions, whether or not the queue has rows. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       TestId="inbox-pane-shortcuts"
                       id="inbox-pane-shortcuts"
                       Title="Keyboard shortcuts (?)"
                       aria-label="Keyboard shortcuts"
                       OnClick="OpenShortcuts"><kbd class="inbox-pane__key">?</kbd></AppButton>
            @* Triage opens the rows one at a time — a way of reading the open
               list, and the pane's main act, so it is the one primary button. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       CssClass="inbox-pane__triage-start inbox-pane__header-act"
                       Title="Decide on the items here one at a time (t; Esc returns to the list)"
                       Disabled="@(!CanStartTriage)"
                       OnClick="StartTriage"
                       TestId="inbox-triage-start">Start triage <kbd class="inbox-pane__key inbox-pane__key--on-primary" aria-hidden="true">T</kbd></AppButton>
        </Actions>
    </SectionHeader>

    @if (_notesOpen)
    {
        @* Notes for the thought in the field: under it, the same editor every
           other body in the product gets, so a list is a button rather than a
           convention. The wrapper hears the keys bubbling out of the editor —
           Ctrl+Enter files, Escape closes — because the editor takes no key
           handler of its own. *@
        <div class="inbox-pane__capture-notes" data-testid="inbox-pane-capture-notes-panel" @onkeydown="OnNotesKeyDownAsync">
            <MarkdownEditor AriaLabel="Notes"
                            Value="@_notes"
                            ValueChanged="v => _notes = v"
                            Id="@NotesInputId"
                            Rows="4"
                            TestId="inbox-pane-capture-notes" />
            <div class="inbox-pane__capture-notes-foot">
                <span class="inbox-pane__hint"><kbd class="inbox-pane__key">Ctrl+Enter</kbd> files the title and the notes · <kbd class="inbox-pane__key">Esc</kbd> closes the notes</span>
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                           Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                           OnClick="CloseNotes"
                           TestId="inbox-pane-capture-notes-close">Close notes</AppButton>
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary"
                           Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                           Disabled="@string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_title)"
                           OnClick="() => FileAsync()"
                           TestId="inbox-pane-capture-submit">Add to Inbox</AppButton>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    @* What the last run said, wearing this pane's own class. A status rather
       than an alert: a run that found nothing new is the ordinary result, and
       the line is replaced by the next run rather than stacked. Alert renders
       nothing at all for an empty message, so there is no branch here — and
       the pane is a flex column for that reason, so an absent line leaves no
       gap behind. *@
    <Alert Message="@CaptureMessage"
           BaseClass="inbox-pane__capture-result"
           Role="status"
           TestId="inbox-pane-capture-result" />

    @if (SourcesShown)
    {
        @* The sources have the pane's whole body, with the way back over them. *@
        <div class="inbox-pane__sources" data-testid="inbox-pane-sources">
            <div class="inbox-pane__sources-bar">
                <AppButton Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                           OnClick="() => State.ShowSources(false)"
                           TestId="inbox-pane-sources-back">Back to Inbox</AppButton>
            </div>
            @Sources
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        @Queue
    }

    @* Every key, in one place a reader can open from the header or with ?. *@
    <Modal Open="@_shortcutsOpen"
           OpenChanged="v => _shortcutsOpen = v"
           Title="Keyboard shortcuts"
           RestoreFocusToId="inbox-pane-shortcuts"
           CssClass="inbox-pane__dialog"
           TestId="inbox-shortcuts-dialog">
        <ChildContent>
            <dl class="inbox-pane__shortcuts" data-testid="inbox-shortcuts-list">
                @foreach (var (keys, action) in Shortcuts)
                {
                    <div class="inbox-pane__shortcut">
                        <dt><kbd class="inbox-pane__key">@keys</kbd></dt>
                        <dd>@action</dd>
                    </div>
                }
            </dl>
            <p class="inbox-pane__shortcuts-note">Keys do nothing while you are typing in a field — a letter there is a letter.</p>
        </ChildContent>
    </Modal>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>Raised when Capture now is pressed. Which sources, and what a
    /// run is, the pane does not know.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnCapture { get; set; }

    /// <summary>True while the host has a run in flight; the button is busy
    /// for as long as this is.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool CaptureRunning { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the last run said, as a sentence. A string rather than a
    /// result type so the Inbox stays free of the Capture context's types — the
    /// host reads the run and writes the line.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CaptureMessage { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What configures the sources Capture now looks at, drawn in the
    /// pane's body when the header's Sources button is pressed. A fragment
    /// rather than a component so the pane never names the Capture context —
    /// the host decides what goes here, and nothing when there is nothing to
    /// configure, in which case there is no Sources button either.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Sources { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The capture field's input, which a capture hands the focus
    /// back to so the next thought can be typed straight away.</summary>
    internal const string CaptureInputId = "inbox-pane-capture-input";

    /// <summary>The notes editor's textarea, which Shift+Enter focuses.</summary>
    internal const string NotesInputId = "inbox-pane-capture-notes-input";

    /// <summary>Whether the body shows the sources rather than the queue.</summary>
    private bool SourcesShown => State.SourcesShown && Sources is not null;

    /// <summary>Triage needs a row to start on and is not started twice.</summary>
    private bool CanStartTriage => State.Loaded && !State.TriageMode && !SourcesShown && State.VisibleItems.Count > 0;

    /// <summary>The queue: one item at full width in triage mode, the side
    /// menu, rows and detail otherwise.</summary>
    private RenderFragment Queue => __builder =>
    {
        if (State.TriageMode)
        {
            @* One item at full width: the side menu and the rows step aside for
               triage's own screen — the item, what the AI noticed, where it would
               go, the four decisions, and what comes next. *@
            <div class="inbox-pane__triage" data-testid="inbox-triage">
                @if (State.SelectedItem is null)
                {
                    <div class="inbox-pane__triage-bar">
                        <AppButton Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                                   Title="Return to the list (Esc)"
                                   OnClick="LeaveTriage"
                                   TestId="inbox-triage-leave">Back to the list</AppButton>
                    </div>
                    <EmptyState Title="@($"Nothing left to triage in {State.SliceName}")"
                                Description="Every item here has been decided on. Go back to the list to see where they went."
                                BaseClass="empty-state inbox-pane__empty-state"
                                TestId="inbox-triage-done" />
                }
                else
                {
                    <InboxTriage @ref="_triage" State="State" OnLeave="LeaveTriage" />
                }
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="inbox-pane__body" data-testid="inbox-pane-body">
                <InboxSideMenu State="State" />

                @if (State.ShowsInboxZero)
                {
                    @* Nothing left waiting and something decided: the session's
                       summary takes the rows' and the detail's place. *@
                    <InboxZero State="State" />
                }
                else
                {
                    @* The list is the fixed pane and the detail takes the rest: a row is a
                       line of metadata that fits a known width, and a detail is a page that
                       wants whatever is left. Anchored at the start for that reason. *@
                    <SplitPane CssClass="inbox-pane__split"
                               Anchor="SplitPaneAnchor.Start"
                               FixedWidthRem="28"
                               MinRem="18"
                               AriaLabel="Inbox list width"
                               TestId="inbox-pane-split"
                               SeparatorTestId="inbox-pane-split-handle">
                        <Start>
                            <InboxItemList State="State" />
                        </Start>
                        <End>
                            <InboxItemDetail @ref="_detail" State="State" />
                        </End>
                    </SplitPane>
                }
            </div>
        }
    };

    private string _title = string.Empty;
    private string _notes = string.Empty;
    private bool _notesOpen;
    private bool _filing;

    private const string PaneId = "inbox-pane";

    private bool _shortcutsOpen;
    private InboxItemDetail? _detail;
    private InboxTriage? _triage;
    private DotNetObjectReference<InboxPane>? _self;

    /// <summary>Whether the item on screen — in triage's view or the column's
    /// detail, whichever is drawn — can still be decided on.</summary>
    private bool CanDecide => State.TriageMode ? _triage?.CanDecide == true : _detail?.CanDecide == true;

    /// <summary>An element to focus once the render that draws it has landed —
    /// the row a step or a triage session ended on, the tag field, the notes
    /// editor or the capture field.</summary>
    private string? _focusAfterRender;

    /// <summary>Every key the pane binds, in the order a session uses them.</summary>
    internal static readonly IReadOnlyList<(string Keys, string Action)> Shortcuts =
    [
        ("t", "Start triage"),
        ("j", "Next item"),
        ("k", "Previous item"),
        ("a", "Archive"),
        ("d", "Defer…"),
        ("l", "Move to list…"),
        ("r", "Move to backlog"),
        ("g", "Tags"),
        ("1–9", "Take the suggestion with that number, from the rows or the item"),
        ("x", "Pick the item for a bulk action"),
        ("u", "Undo the last decision"),
        ("Esc", "Back to the list, from triage"),
        ("?", "These shortcuts"),
    ];

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        State.Changed += HandleStateChanged;
    }

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            _self = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneShortcuts.register", PaneId, _self);
        }

        if (_focusAfterRender is { } id)
        {
            _focusAfterRender = null;
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", id, false);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>One shortcut, already judged by components.js to be a shortcut
    /// rather than typing. An act the detail would not offer for this item — a
    /// decided item has no Archive — is not offered by its key either.</summary>
    [JSInvokable]
    public async Task OnShortcutAsync(string key)
    {
        await InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            // With the sources shown the rows are not on screen, and a key that
            // decided an item the reader cannot see would be a surprise.
            if (SourcesShown && key != "?") return;

            // The inbox-zero screen has no detail on it, so the keys that act
            // on the open item have nothing to act on; U and ? still work.
            if (State.ShowsInboxZero && key is "a" or "r" or "d" or "l" or "g" or "x") return;

            switch (key)
            {
                case "j":
                case "k":
                    State.Step(key == "j" ? 1 : -1);
                    if (!State.TriageMode && State.SelectedItemId is { } id) _focusAfterRender = InboxItemList.RowId(id);
                    break;
                case "t" when CanStartTriage:
                    StartTriage();
                    break;
                case "a" when CanDecide:
                    await State.ArchiveAsync();
                    break;
                case "r" when CanDecide:
                    // In triage the repositories ticked there go with the route.
                    await (State.TriageMode ? State.RouteFromTriageAsync() : State.RouteToBacklogAsync());
                    break;
                case "d":
                    if (State.TriageMode) _triage?.OpenDefer();
                    else _detail?.OpenDefer();
                    break;
                case "l":
                    if (State.TriageMode) _triage?.OpenMove();
                    else _detail?.OpenMove();
                    break;
                case "g" when CanDecide:
                    // Triage draws its tag field under the same id the detail does.
                    _focusAfterRender = InboxItemDetail.TagsInputId;
                    break;
                case "x" when State.SelectedItemId is { } picked && State.TriagePosition >= 0:
                    State.TogglePicked(picked, !State.IsPicked(picked), range: false);
                    break;
                case "u" or "U":
                    await State.UndoLatestAsync();
                    break;
                case "?":
                    _shortcutsOpen = true;
                    break;
                case "Escape" when State.TriageMode:
                    LeaveTriage();
                    break;
                default:
                    return;
            }

            StateHasChanged();
        });
    }

    private void OpenShortcuts() => _shortcutsOpen = true;

    /// <summary>One item at a time, from the chosen row or the first.</summary>
    private void StartTriage()
    {
        if (!CanStartTriage) return;

        State.SetTriageMode(true);
    }

    /// <summary>Back to the columns, on the row the session stopped at.</summary>
    private void LeaveTriage()
    {
        State.SetTriageMode(false);
        if (State.SelectedItemId is { } id && State.TriagePosition >= 0) _focusAfterRender = InboxItemList.RowId(id);
    }

    // --- The capture field -----------------------------------------------------

    /// <summary>The field's own keys. Enter files what is typed — with the notes
    /// too when they are open, so nothing written under the field is dropped —
    /// Shift+Enter opens the notes, Ctrl+Enter files both, and Escape closes the
    /// notes. Every other key is the field's to type.</summary>
    private async Task OnCaptureKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "Enter" when e.ShiftKey:
                OpenNotes();
                break;
            case "Enter" when e.AltKey:
                break;
            case "Enter":
                await FileAsync();
                break;
            case "Escape" when _notesOpen:
                CloseNotes();
                break;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The notes editor's keys, heard as they bubble out of it:
    /// Ctrl+Enter files the title and the notes, Escape closes the notes and
    /// keeps the title. What was written in them goes with them: notes kept
    /// out of sight would be filed with the next Enter without anybody seeing
    /// them.</summary>
    private async Task OnNotesKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        switch (e.Key)
        {
            case "Enter" when e.CtrlKey || e.MetaKey:
                await FileAsync();
                break;
            case "Escape":
                CloseNotes();
                _notes = string.Empty;
                _focusAfterRender = CaptureInputId;
                break;
        }
    }

    private void OpenNotes()
    {
        _notesOpen = true;
        _focusAfterRender = NotesInputId;
    }

    private void CloseNotes() => _notesOpen = false;

    /// <summary>Files the title, with the notes when they are open, through the
    /// state. Nothing is filed without a title, because the module refuses an
    /// item without one. Emptied only once the item is kept — a refusal is a
    /// toast, and the words typed are still in the field to try again with —
    /// and only where nothing new was typed while the item was being kept, so
    /// the next thought started during the save is not wiped. A capture made
    /// with the sources shown brings the queue back, so the new row is seen.</summary>
    private async Task FileAsync()
    {
        if (_filing || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_title)) return;

        _filing = true;
        try
        {
            var title = _title;
            var notes = _notesOpen ? _notes : string.Empty;
            var kept = await State.CaptureAsync(new InboxCapture(title.Trim(), notes.Trim()));
            if (!kept) return;

            if (_title == title) _title = string.Empty;
            if (_notes == notes)
            {
                _notes = string.Empty;
                _notesOpen = false;
            }

            State.ShowSources(false);
            _focusAfterRender = CaptureInputId;
        }
        finally
        {
            _filing = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The state raises from whichever thread finished the store call;
    /// the render is marshalled onto the renderer's.</summary>
    private void HandleStateChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        State.Changed -= HandleStateChanged;

        if (_self is null) return;

        try
        {
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogPaneShortcuts.unregister", PaneId);
        }
        catch (JSDisconnectedException)
        {
            // The circuit is gone, and the page with it; there is nothing left to unregister from.
        }

        _self.Dispose();
    }
}
